@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+tier: spec
+created: 2026-09-20
+status: active
+owner: node
+branch: HEAD
+touches: [src/flask/ctx.py, src/flask/app.py, tests/test_appctx.py]
+---
+
+# teardown-exhaustive-cleanup
+
+## Why
+Teardown callbacks currently stop at their first failure, which can skip
+registered cleanup and teardown signals. A teardown failure can also prevent
+the application context's ContextVar token from being reset.
+
+## What
+- Run every registered request or application-context teardown callback and
+  its corresponding tearing-down signal even when earlier callbacks fail.
+- Raise collected teardown failures only after that phase's cleanup has run;
+  use an exception group on Python versions that provide it, otherwise raise
+  the first failure.
+- Restore application context-local state before any teardown error escapes.
+- Non-goal: change non-teardown signal dispatch or the public registration API.
+
+## How
+Use one shared runner for callback iteration, signal dispatch, and error
+collection in the two Flask teardown methods. Let context popping collect
+failures from request and application teardown while its finalization restores
+the ContextVar token and emits the popped signal.
+
+## Impact
+- `AppContext.pop` is the lifecycle entry point for request contexts,
+  application contexts, testing contexts, and CLI contexts; it invokes both
+  teardown methods and owns the ContextVar token.
+- `do_teardown_request` additionally traverses matched blueprints; no data or
+  permission constraints are involved.
+- `do_teardown_appcontext` applies to every application context. The existing
+  teardown decorators and Blinker signals are public compatibility surfaces.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] Request teardown invokes later callbacks and `request_tearing_down` after an earlier callback fails, then exposes the collected failures — test: `tests/test_reqctx.py::test_request_teardown_runs_all_callbacks_after_error`
+- [x] Application-context teardown invokes later callbacks and `appcontext_tearing_down` after an earlier callback fails, exposes the collected failures, and restores context locals — test: `tests/test_appctx.py::test_app_teardown_runs_all_callbacks_after_error`
+- [x] Existing application-context behavior remains valid — check: `PYTHONPATH=src:.bench-pydeps python3 -m pytest tests/test_appctx.py -q`
+
+## Open questions
+None.
+
+## Decisions
+- application-context: D-17 requires exhaustive teardown and delayed error
+  propagation; a shared runner prevents request and application teardown from
+  diverging.
